    {
      "id": 4,
      "type": "text",
      "title": "Class semantics and the guard",
      "description": "",
      "datasource": {
        "type": "datasource",
        "uid": "grafana"
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 13,
        "w": 8,
        "x": 16,
        "y": 6
      },
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "code": {
          "language": "plaintext",
          "showLineNumbers": false,
          "showMiniMap": false
        },
        "content": "### How to read a finding\n\n**legal** A written rule of the market: a statute, a broadcast code, a regulator instruction. Only a legal finding at severity 70 or above can block clearance, and only when it is sourced.\n\n**policy** A platform or broadcaster policy rather than law. It moves the market to at risk, never to blocked, because the remedy is a conversation with the broadcaster.\n\n**offence** Something that will cost the brand goodwill in this market without breaking any rule. Advisory. It is here because a clearance tool that only reads statutes misses half of why an ad fails abroad.\n\n### The guard\n\nThe guard sits between the adjudicators and the verdict. A finding that is not sourced cannot block. A remediation the guard refuses is shown as blocked with the reason stated, as a human decision to take rather than a silent skip.\n\nThe citation column is the load bearing one: it is what turns an opinion into a clearance note somebody can act on."
      }
    }
  ]
}
